{"path": "summary.md", "content": "# Latent Camera — Architectural & Functional Summary

`source.kt` implements a single-activity Android camera app (`com.pixelary.latent`) built with Jetpack Compose, Camera2, and OpenGL ES 3.0. `MainActivity` is a thin host that keeps the screen on, enables edge-to-edge rendering, and forwards hardware volume-key presses (and an exported-scope-free `TOGGLE_VIDEO` broadcast receiver) into a `SharedFlow` consumed by `MainScreen`. `MainScreen` is the orchestration hub: it manages runtime permissions (camera, audio, location), camera/lens enumeration and selection, and a large set of Compose state for exposure, ISO/shutter, zoom, white balance locking, denoising/false-color/highlight-recovery shader stages, histogram, comparison mode, and debug telemetry. It delegates actual work to dedicated collaborators: `CameraProvider` (device enumeration and lens metadata), `SensorCapture` (Camera2 device/session, RAW `ImageReader` capture, metadata callbacks, manual ISO/shutter/AE-lock/OIS/flicker control), `LivePreview` (the GL renderer that runs the RAW→image ISP shader pipeline, capture readback, and video recording), `CaptureVideo` (encoder), `LocationTracker`, and `PerformanceOptimizer` (ADPF hint sessions, sustained performance mode, and promotion of render/ISP/capture threads to urgent-display priority).

The UI is a full-screen Compose `Scaffold` with an `AndroidView`-embedded `GLSurfaceView` (rendered on demand in `RENDERMODE_WHEN_DIRTY` for power efficiency), overlaid with status readouts, a zoom slider, physical-lens/front-camera selectors, a live "Google Camera"/RAW preview button, a settings sheet (`CameraSelectionOverlay`), and a shutter control whose gesture logic distinguishes tap (photo) from long-press (video), with upward drag-to-lock recording. Photos are captured by requesting a GL readback and then, on a two-thread background executor, converting the buffer to a JPEG, writing EXIF (make/model/ISO/exposure/focal/aperture/GPS), and inserting into `MediaStore` under `DCIM/Camera`; videos are recorded to app-internal MP4s, then moved to the gallery on a background thread. Lifecycle is handled explicitly: on pause/stop the receiver is unregistered, recording is stopped and saved, and the camera, preview, and GL surface are torn down; on resume the receiver is re-registered and, crucially, all sensor-specific parameters (orientation, CFA pattern, black/white levels, distortion, resolution) are pushed into `LivePreview` *before* reopening the camera, so the first processed frame is already correct and no stale state leaks across lens switches. Performance instrumentation (processing time, battery draw, thermal status, build age, per-stage capture timings) is optionally surfaced via the debug overlay and logs."}

